WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:07.459
The stars of Hardcore Pawn will tell you that
a story comes with every item that passes

00:00:07.459 --> 00:00:10.129
through their shop's doors, and they're not
kidding.

00:00:10.129 --> 00:00:14.500
Only a small fraction of those stories actually
makes it on the air, however.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:20.119
The rest are left on the cutting room floor,
to later be dusted off and presented here.

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:23.380
Les Gold once owned Dr. Kevorkian's van

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.759
In one of the more morbid transactions in
pawn shop entertainment history, American

00:00:27.759 --> 00:00:34.780
Jewelry &amp; Loan once purchased Dr. Jack Kevorkian's
van, according to the New York Daily News.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.930
Kevorkian performed about 130 assisted suicides
in the 1968 Volkswagen Minibus, which Les

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:45.560
Gold bought from a scrapyard in 1997 for $20,000.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.719
Gold then owned the "Deathmobile," as it's
known, for almost 20 years, until he sold

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:54.219
it for a $5,000 profit in 2014 because it
took up "too much room."

00:00:54.219 --> 00:01:01.039
Amazingly, Gold sold the van to another reality
TV show star: Zac Bagans from Ghost Adventures.

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:02.249
Spooky coincidence?

00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:04.540
Or reality TV synergy?

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.680
Seth Gold: Man of the people

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.140
In December 2014, Michigan state senators
wanted to introduce a bill that allowed pawn

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.200
shops to increase the interest they charged
on pawned items by 20 percent per month.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.030
While this sounds like something shop owners
would support wholeheartedly, Seth Gold of

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.970
American Jewelry &amp; Loan actually opposed the
measure.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:29.530
"Our business is predicated on people getting
their stuff back, redeeming their merchandise.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:33.970
In fact, on a national average, 80 to 90 percent
of all customers that get loans with us get

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:34.970
their merchandise back.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.729
If you stretch that interest rate and make
it higher, well the default rate's gonna explode."

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:43.100
Fortunately for Gold's customers, it seems
the bill never actually became a law.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.630
They're patriots

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:50.300
According to Detroit's WDIV news, back in
2012, Les and Seth helped the U.S. Secret

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:55.390
Service nab Kenny "Boom" Smith, who attempted
to sell some bogus hundred dollar bills and

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:58.110
a counterfeiting machine to the famous pawn
shop.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.670
Even more ridiculous?

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.540
Smith signed a waiver to appear on the show.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:07.180
It's unlikely that Smith has a future in television,
except maybe an appearance on COPS.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.009
Ashley Gold Broad runs an honest shop

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:15.290
In an interview with the Las Vegas Sun, Ashley
Gold Broad told a story from a few years earlier,

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:19.510
when a woman came into the shop asking $100
for a bag of stones.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:24.360
Broad looked through the bag and found a Burmese
ruby, which a certified gemologist appraised

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.340
at $10,000.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.730
Rather than take advantage of her find, Broad
did the honest thing and gave the woman ten

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:31.730
grand.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:36.320
While some would see that as an amazingly
kind gesture, Broad insists it's just good

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.440
business.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.019
They have celebrity clientele

00:02:39.019 --> 00:02:43.900
So American Jewelry &amp; Loan may not have hundreds
of celebs stepping through their doors every

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.590
day, but the one that can be verified is impressive.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:52.080
According to the Detroit Free Press, Breaking
Bad's Aaron Paul once visited the store in

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.150
search of a gold bar with an inscription reading,
"Yeah, B****!"

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:01.190
Of the event, Ashley Gold Broad said, "I'm
able to accommodate a lot of people at the

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:02.650
shop, but that one didn't happen."

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.530
Sorry, Jesse.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.670
One episode has a tragic postscript

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:12.379
American Jewelry &amp; Loan may not be the safest
place to visit, even by pawn shop standards.

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:17.690
Detroit resident David Kapuscinski, who made
a memorable appearance on the show in 2013,

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:21.720
was killed two years later in a confrontation
with police.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.010
According to reports, Kapuscinski failed to
obey verbal orders from officers called to

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:31.159
his apartment during a domestic disturbance,
which resulted in repeated tasering.

00:03:31.159 --> 00:03:35.680
Kapuscinski's girlfriend claims it was a misunderstanding,
and told reporters that the thick-headed bravado

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.980
he displayed on the show was just part of
a setup for the series.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.879
Why are they always in the same clothes?

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:46.569
Ever wonder why the main cast of the show
is often seen wearing the same outfit on air?

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:52.540
Well, according to an April 2016 "AMA" chat
with Les and Seth Gold on Reddit, it has less

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:56.860
to do with being clueless to today's trends
and more to do with continuity.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:57.860
Les revealed:

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:01.000
"I have twelve of the white sweaters at least.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.099
We wear the same clothing day to day for continuity
purposes on the show."

00:04:05.099 --> 00:04:10.599
The same apparently applies to Seth, who he
says "only has one outfit, too."

00:04:10.599 --> 00:04:11.599
Unscripted

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:16.739
Over the years, many reality TV shows have
been accused of or exposed as being fake.

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:17.840
What about Hardcore Pawn?

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.130
If Seth is to be believed, the show is 100
percent real, saying during the AMA:

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.640
"We're pawnbrokers, not actors.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.720
The truth is, these are real customers.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.000
Also, the stuff you see us buy on the show?

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.440
You can see that it's real, too, because we
have things you've seen on the show for sale

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:34.980
on our showroom floor."

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.070
Thanks for watching!

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:40.880
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to see more
videos like the one you just saw.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.320
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Hardcore Pawn fact surprised you the most...

